import { len, print, push, !, == } from builtins;

// Regression test for corruption of a `mut` *struct* binding reassigned from a
// call on itself inside a loop. The `mut str` sibling of this bug is
// `strings/mut_str_reassigned_in_loop`.
//
// A struct-returning call gets one hidden-sret buffer **per call site**, and a
// `mut` binding of composite type holds a *pointer* to its value rather than the
// value inline. So after the first iteration of
//
//     while (!s.done) { set s = step(s); }
//
// `s` points *at* that sret buffer, and the next iteration's call reads its
// argument out of the very buffer it is writing its result into. The IR for
// `run` shows it plainly: an 8-byte slot holds `s`, while the 24-byte sret
// buffer is allocated once in the entry block and re-addressed on every trip
// through the loop body.
//
// Two sequential `set s = step(s);` statements are fine — separate call sites
// get separate sret buffers, so nothing aliases. The trigger is a loop plus a
// callee that rebuilds a refcounted field; it needs no variant, no matched
// payload, no helper fn and no empty initial array, and it reproduces under AOT
// as well as the JIT, so it is codegen rather than the harness.
//
// One iteration is deliberate. It corrupts `acc`'s length quietly — the count
// comes back as whatever the reused buffer happened to hold, varying run to run
// — whereas letting the loop run longer (`done: 2 <= s.acc.len()`) turns the
// same corruption into a segfault. The result is reported as a *classification*
// rather than the raw count so this case's output stays deterministic in both
// the broken and the fixed state.
//
// The fix: a composite `mut` binding's slot owns its value's fields, and `set`
// copies the incoming value into a buffer belonging to that `set` before
// repointing the slot — retain the incoming fields, release the outgoing ones,
// then copy. See the `LetMut`/`Assign` composite arms in `aipl-codegen`.
//
// `doc.aipl`'s printer no longer needs its workaround: it keeps its output
// accumulator in a `mut` binding outside the state struct — that is now a
// stylistic choice rather than a necessity.

struct St { acc: str[], done: bool }

fn pushed(self: str[], x: str) -> str[] {
    mut a = self;
    set a.push(x);
    a
}

fn step(s: St) -> St { St { acc: s.acc.pushed("k"), done: true } }

fn run() -> u64 {
    mut s = St { acc: [], done: false };
    while (!s.done) {
        set s = step(s);
    }
    s.acc.len()
}

fn main() !prints {
    // Exactly one iteration runs, pushing exactly one element.
    print(if (run() == 1) { "one element" } else { "corrupted" });
}
--- stdout ---
one element
